## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of sliding-window streaming algorithms, mergeable sketches, and amortized cost analysis under tight memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window distinct counting
- HyperLogLog mergeability
- Bucketization for windowing
- Memory vs error trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingHLL {
  constructor(K, m) {
    this.hlls = Array.from({length: K}, () => new HyperLogLog(m));
    this.idx = 0;
  }
  update(batch) {
    const i = this.idx % this.hlls.length;
    for (const id of batch) this.hlls[i].add(id);
    this.idx++;
  }
  estimate() {
    const merged = HyperLogLog.merge(this.hlls);
    return merged.estimate();
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental connectivity intuition, DSU basics, and amortized analysis for real-world batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Union-Find with path compression and union by rank
- Alpha(n)
- Batch vs online update trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.components = n;
  }
  find(x){ return this.parent[x]===x? x: this.find(this.parent[x]); }
  union(a,b){ let ra=this.find(a), rb=this.find(b); if(ra===rb) return false; if(this.rank[ra]<this.rank[rb])[ra,rb]=[rb,ra]; this.parent[rb]=ra; if(this.rank[ra]===this.rank[rb]) this.rank[ra]++; this.components--; return true; }
}
```

